window.BAHR_DATA=window.BAHR_DATA||{};window.BAHR_DATA["passages-104"]=[{"id":"K104.01","q":"104:1–9","s":104,"surah":"al-Humazah","surah_ar":"الهمزة","title":"al-Humazah · Heading and verses","cairo":"K IV:794–796 · T XVI:568–576","source_page_first":794,"source_page_last":796,"chunk":"passages-104","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"Heading and verses [IV:794]","html":"<p>Meccan; nine verses. [The DKA heading additionally places its revelation after al-Qiyāmah.]\nIn the name of God, the All-Merciful, the Merciful.</p><p>Woe to every backbiter and slanderer, (1) who amasses wealth and counts it over, (2) thinking that his wealth has made him immortal. (3) No indeed! He will surely be cast into the Crusher. (4) And what will make you know what the Crusher is? (5) God’s kindled fire, (6) which rises over the hearts. (7) It is closed over them, (8) amid extended pillars. (9)</p>","author":"kashshaf","source_id":"K104.01"}],"types":["exposition","language","theology","reports"],"ar":"مكية، وآياتها 9 [نزلت بعد القيامة] بسم الله الرحمن الرحيم","layers":[{"id":"K104.01","heading":"K104.01 — Heading and verses [IV:794]","text":"Meccan; nine verses. [The DKA heading additionally places its revelation after al-Qiyāmah.]\nIn the name of God, the All-Merciful, the Merciful.\n\nWoe to every backbiter and slanderer, (1) who amasses wealth and counts it over, (2) thinking that his wealth has made him immortal. (3) No indeed! He will surely be cast into the Crusher. (4) And what will make you know what the Crusher is? (5) God’s kindled fire, (6) which rises over the hearts. (7) It is closed over them, (8) amid extended pillars. (9)","title":"Heading and verses [IV:794]","author":"kashshaf","surah":104,"ar":"مكية، وآياتها 9 [نزلت بعد القيامة] بسم الله الرحمن الرحيم","html":"<p>Meccan; nine verses. [The DKA heading additionally places its revelation after al-Qiyāmah.]\nIn the name of God, the All-Merciful, the Merciful.</p><p>Woe to every backbiter and slanderer, (1) who amasses wealth and counts it over, (2) thinking that his wealth has made him immortal. (3) No indeed! He will surely be cast into the Crusher. (4) And what will make you know what the Crusher is? (5) God’s kindled fire, (6) which rises over the hearts. (7) It is closed over them, (8) amid extended pillars. (9)</p>","lemma":"","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/middle-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}}],"cairo_notes":[],"verses":[{"key":"104:1","number":1,"ar":"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَـٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ وَيْلٌ لِّكُلِّ هُمَزَةٍ لُّمَزَةٍ","en":"Woe unto every slandering traducer"},{"key":"104:2","number":2,"ar":"ٱلَّذِى جَمَعَ مَالًا وَعَدَّدَهُۥ","en":"Who hath gathered wealth (of this world) and arranged it"},{"key":"104:3","number":3,"ar":"يَحْسَبُ أَنَّ مَالَهُۥٓ أَخْلَدَهُۥ","en":"He thinketh that his wealth will render him immortal"},{"key":"104:4","number":4,"ar":"كَلَّا ۖ لَيُنۢبَذَنَّ فِى ٱلْحُطَمَةِ","en":"Nay, but verily he will be flung to the Consuming One"},{"key":"104:5","number":5,"ar":"وَمَآ أَدْرَىٰكَ مَا ٱلْحُطَمَةُ","en":"Ah, what will convey unto thee what the Consuming One is"},{"key":"104:6","number":6,"ar":"نَارُ ٱللَّهِ ٱلْمُوقَدَةُ","en":"(It is) the fire of Allah, kindled"},{"key":"104:7","number":7,"ar":"ٱلَّتِى تَطَّلِعُ عَلَى ٱلْأَفْـِٔدَةِ","en":"Which leapeth up over the hearts (of men)"},{"key":"104:8","number":8,"ar":"إِنَّهَا عَلَيْهِم مُّؤْصَدَةٌ","en":"Lo! it is closed in on them"},{"key":"104:9","number":9,"ar":"فِى عَمَدٍ مُّمَدَّدَةٍۭ","en":"In outstretched columns"}],"quran_ar":"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَـٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ وَيْلٌ لِّكُلِّ هُمَزَةٍ لُّمَزَةٍ ﴿1﴾ ٱلَّذِى جَمَعَ مَالًا وَعَدَّدَهُۥ ﴿2﴾ يَحْسَبُ أَنَّ مَالَهُۥٓ أَخْلَدَهُۥ ﴿3﴾ كَلَّا ۖ لَيُنۢبَذَنَّ فِى ٱلْحُطَمَةِ ﴿4﴾ وَمَآ أَدْرَىٰكَ مَا ٱلْحُطَمَةُ ﴿5﴾ نَارُ ٱللَّهِ ٱلْمُوقَدَةُ ﴿6﴾ ٱلَّتِى تَطَّلِعُ عَلَى ٱلْأَفْـِٔدَةِ ﴿7﴾ إِنَّهَا عَلَيْهِم مُّؤْصَدَةٌ ﴿8﴾ فِى عَمَدٍ مُّمَدَّدَةٍۭ ﴿9﴾","quran_en":"1. Woe unto every slandering traducer 2. Who hath gathered wealth (of this world) and arranged it 3. He thinketh that his wealth will render him immortal 4. Nay, but verily he will be flung to the Consuming One 5. Ah, what will convey unto thee what the Consuming One is 6. (It is) the fire of Allah, kindled 7. Which leapeth up over the hearts (of men) 8. Lo! it is closed in on them 9. In outstretched columns"},{"id":"K104.02","q":"104:1","s":104,"surah":"al-Humazah","surah_ar":"الهمزة","title":"al-Humazah · Backbiting, ridicule, and a general warning","cairo":"K IV:794–796 · T XVI:568–576","source_page_first":794,"source_page_last":796,"chunk":"passages-104","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"Backbiting, ridicule, and a general warning [IV:794–795]","html":"<p>Hamz means breaking, like hazm (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">هزم</bdi>); lamz (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">لمز</bdi>) means piercing. One says lamazahu (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">لمزه</bdi>) and lahazahu (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">لهزه</bdi>), “he stabbed him.” What is intended is breaking people’s reputations, diminishing them, backbiting them, and attacking them. The pattern fuʿala (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فُعَلة</bdi>) indicates that this is a habit to which the person has become addicted. Comparable forms are luʿana (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">لُعَنة</bdi>), a habitual curser, and ḍuḥaka (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ضُحَكة</bdi>), a habitual laugher. The poet says:\n“And when I am absent, you are the backbiter, the slanderer.”</p><p>The reading waylun li-l-humazati l-lumaza, “woe to the backbiter, the slanderer,” is also transmitted. Another reading has humza lumza with the mīm (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">م</bdi>) quiescent: this is the laughing-stock who produces outlandish acts and comical displays, and is laughed at and abused.</p><p>It is said to have been revealed concerning al-Akhnas ibn Sharīq, whose habit was backbiting and defamation; or concerning Umayya ibn Khalaf; or al-Walīd ibn al-Mughīra and his backbiting and disparaging God’s Messenger. The occasion may be particular while the threat is general, so as to include everyone who undertakes this ugly conduct, and to operate as an indirect allusion to the person concerning whom it was revealed. That is more restraining and more wounding to him.</p>","author":"kashshaf","source_id":"K104.02"},{"type":"exposition","title":"Breaking reputations and the correspondence of punishment [XVI:568–569]","html":"<p>Meccan; nine verses. In the name of God, the All-Merciful, the Merciful.</p><p>On “hamz (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">همز</bdi>) means breaking”: according to some, hamz (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">همز</bdi>) is like squeezing with the hand. One says, “I squeezed the thing in my palm”; from this comes hamz (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">همز</bdi>) in letters. Hamz of a person is backbiting him. One says hāmiz (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">هامز</bdi>), hammāz (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">همّاز</bdi>), and humaza of a man.</p><p>On “diminishing them,” al-Jawharī says: “Ghaḍḍa minhu yaghuḍḍu (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">يغضّ</bdi>), with ḍamma (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ضَمّة</bdi>) in the imperfect, means that he lowered and diminished his standing.” Others relate to it lowering the gaze and voice, and restraining blame.</p><p>On “the pattern fuʿala (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فُعَلة</bdi>) indicates that this is a habit,” al-Intiṣāf says: “How fine is the correspondence of humaza and lumaza with ḥuṭama (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">حُطَمة</bdi>)! Having marked him with this description and with a form indicating rootedness and firmness, He threatens him with this corresponding attribute, so that deed and recompense balance one another.”</p><p>I say: there is another subtle correspondence. Hamz includes breaking reputations; ḥaṭm (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">حطم</bdi>) includes breaking ribs. Nabdh, casting away, conveys contempt and belittlement, since the man imagined himself to belong among the honoured. Under His saying, ‘We seized him and his troops and cast them into the sea’ [28:40], he says: “To express contempt for them and belittle their number, He likens them to little pebbles which someone takes in his palm and throws into the sea.” Al-Wāḥidī transmits from Muqātil: “It crushes bones and consumes flesh until it assails the hearts.”</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T104.01"},{"type":"exposition","title":"The poetic example and strange utterances [XVI:569–570]","html":"<p>On “And when I am absent, you are the backbiter, the slanderer”: it is said that the opening is:\n“You proffer affection for me, falsely, when you meet me.”</p><p>Al-Zajjāj cites these words of Ziyād al-Aʿjam:\n“When I meet you, you bare your teeth in a smile despite your resentment;\nwhen I am absent, you are the backbiter, the slanderer.”</p><p>Ibn al-Sikkīt says: “Kashr means smiling. Kashara al-rajulu, iftarra, and ibtasama all mean that his teeth become visible.”</p><p>On “outlandish acts,” al-Asās says: “Among figurative uses is ‘So-and-so is devoted to awābid (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أوابد</bdi>) of speech,’ meaning its strange expressions, and to awābid (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أوابد</bdi>) of poetry, those poems whose excellence is unmatched.”</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T104.02"},{"type":"exposition","title":"General language and indirect rebuke [XVI:570–571]","html":"<p>On “the occasion may be particular while the threat is general”: the Imām transmits from al-Farrāʾ: “An expression’s being general does not contradict a particular person’s being intended. For instance, someone says to you, ‘I have never visited you,’ and you say, ‘Everyone who has not visited me, I will not visit.’ In legal theory this is called restricting a general expression by a contextual indication of customary usage.”</p><p>On “to operate as an indirect allusion to the person concerning whom it was revealed”: if that person is al-Akhnas, Umayya, or al-Walīd, and the wording is made general as an indirect rebuke, this is more restraining and wounding. His name has not been explicitly stated so that he would put on the leopard’s hide against the person confronting him. Instead it prompts him to reflect on his own state: has he entered this general category before everyone else by backbiting the best of creation and diminishing his due? Al-Asās says: “Nakaytu fī (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">في</bdi>) l-ʿaduwwi nikāyatan means I inflicted many wounds upon the enemy. One says, ‘So-and-so inflicts little hurt but complains at length.’”</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T104.03"}],"types":["exposition","language","theology","reports"],"ar":"الهمز: الكسر، كالهزم. واللمز: الطعن. يقال: لمزه ولهزه طعنه، والمراد: الكسر من أعراض الناس والغض منهم، واغتيابهم، والطعن فيهم وبناء «فعلة» يدل على أن ذلك عادة منه قد ضرى بها. ونحوهما: اللعنة والضحكة. قال: وإن أغيب فأنت الهامز اللمزه وقرئ: ويل للهمزة اللمزة. وقرئ: ويل لكل همزة لمزة، بسكون الميم: وهو المسخرة الذي يأتى بالأوابد والأضاحيك فيضحك منه ويشتم. وقيل: نزلت في الأخنس بن شريق وكانت عادته الغيبة والوقيعة. وقيل: في أمية بن خلف. وقيل: في الوليد بن المغيرة واغتيابه لرسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم وغضه منه. ويجوز أن يكون السبب خاصا والوعيد عاما، ليتناول كل من باشر ذلك القبيح، وليكون جاريا مجرى التعريض بالوارد فيه، فإن ذلك أزجر له وأنكى فيه\n\nمكية، تسع آيات\n\nبسم الله الرحمن الرحيم\n\nقوله: (الهُمز: الكسر)، عن بعضهم: الهمز كالعصر باليد، [يقال]: همزت الشيء في كفي، ومنه: الهمز في الحروف. وهمز الإنسان: اغتيابه، يقال: رجل هامز وهماز وهُمزة.\n\nقوله: (والغض منهم)، الجوهري: \"وغض منه يُغض بالضم، أي: وضع ونقص من قدره\". وعن غيره: منه غض الطرف والصوت: خفضهما، وغض الملامة: كفها.\n\nقوله: (وبناء فُعلة يدل على أن ذلك عادة منه)، الانتصاف: \"ما أحسن مُقابلة الهُمزة واللُّمزة بالحُطمة، لأنه لما وسمه بهذه السمة، وبما يدل على الرسوخ والتمكن، توعد فيها بهذه الصفة ليحصل التعادل بين الفعل والجزاء\".\n\nوقلت: فيه لطيفة أخرى من حيث التعادل، وهي أن الهمز فيه معنى الكسر من الأعراض، والحطم فيه معنى الكسر من الأضلاع، والنبذ فيه استحقار واستقلال، لأنه كان يزعم أنه من أهل الكرامة، قال في قوله تعالى: ﴿فَأَخَذْنَاهُ وَجُنُودَهُ فَنَبَذْنَاهُمْ فِي الْيَمِّ﴾ [القصص: 40]: \"شبههم استحقاراً لهم واستقلالاً لعددهم، بحصيات أخذهن آخذ في كفه فطرحهن في البحر\". روى الواحدي عن مقاتل: \"هي تُحطم العظام، وتأكل اللحوم حتى تهجم على القلوب\".\n\nقوله: (وإن أُغَيب فأنت الهامز اللُّمزة)، قيل: أوله:\n\nتُدلي بودي إذا لاقيتني كذباً\n\nوأنشد الزجاج لزياد الأعجم:\n\nإذا لقيتك عن سُخط تُكاشرني وإن تغيبت كنت الهامز اللُّمزه\n\nابن السِّكيت: \"الكشر: التبسم، يقال: كشر الرجل وافتر وابتسم، كل ذلك تبدو منه الأسنان\".\n\nقوله: (بالأوابد)، الأساس: \"ومن المجاز: فلان مولع بأوابد الكلام، وهي غرائبه، وبأوابد الشِّعر، وهي التي لا تُشاكل جودة\".\n\nقوله: (ويجوز أن يكون السبب خاصاً والوعيد عاماً)، روى الإمام عن الفراء أنه قال: \"كون اللفظ عاماً، لا ينافي أن يكون المراد منه شخصاً معيناً، كما أن إنساناً لو قال لك: لم أزرك أبداً، فتقول: كل من لم يزرني لا أزوره، وهو المسمى في \"أصول الفقه\" بتخصيص العام بقرينة العُرف\".\n\nقوله: (وليكون جارياً مجرى التعريض بالوارد فيه)، يعني: إذا كان الوارد منه الأخنس أو أمية أو الوليد، ويجاء باللفظ على العموم تعريضاً، كان أزجر له وأنكى فيه، إذ لم يصرح باسمه حتى يلبس لمن كافحه به جلد النمر، بل يبعثه على الفكر في أحوال نفسه، وأنه هل دخل في هذا العام أول الناس بما اغتاب به خير البرية ونقص من حقه؟ الأساس: \"نكيت في العدو نكاية: إذا أكثرت الجراح فيهم، يقال: فلان قليل النكاية طويل الشكاية\".","layers":[{"id":"K104.02","heading":"K104.02 — Backbiting, ridicule, and a general warning [IV:794–795]","text":"Hamz means breaking, like hazm (هزم); lamz (لمز) means piercing. One says lamazahu (لمزه) and lahazahu (لهزه), “he stabbed him.” What is intended is breaking people’s reputations, diminishing them, backbiting them, and attacking them. The pattern fuʿala (فُعَلة) indicates that this is a habit to which the person has become addicted. Comparable forms are luʿana (لُعَنة), a habitual curser, and ḍuḥaka (ضُحَكة), a habitual laugher. The poet says:\n“And when I am absent, you are the backbiter, the slanderer.”\n\nThe reading waylun li-l-humazati l-lumaza, “woe to the backbiter, the slanderer,” is also transmitted. Another reading has humza lumza with the mīm (م) quiescent: this is the laughing-stock who produces outlandish acts and comical displays, and is laughed at and abused.\n\nIt is said to have been revealed concerning al-Akhnas ibn Sharīq, whose habit was backbiting and defamation; or concerning Umayya ibn Khalaf; or al-Walīd ibn al-Mughīra and his backbiting and disparaging God’s Messenger. The occasion may be particular while the threat is general, so as to include everyone who undertakes this ugly conduct, and to operate as an indirect allusion to the person concerning whom it was revealed. That is more restraining and more wounding to him.","title":"Backbiting, ridicule, and a general warning [IV:794–795]","author":"kashshaf","surah":104,"ar":"الهمز: الكسر، كالهزم. واللمز: الطعن. يقال: لمزه ولهزه طعنه، والمراد: الكسر من أعراض الناس والغض منهم، واغتيابهم، والطعن فيهم وبناء «فعلة» يدل على أن ذلك عادة منه قد ضرى بها. ونحوهما: اللعنة والضحكة. قال: وإن أغيب فأنت الهامز اللمزه وقرئ: ويل للهمزة اللمزة. وقرئ: ويل لكل همزة لمزة، بسكون الميم: وهو المسخرة الذي يأتى بالأوابد والأضاحيك فيضحك منه ويشتم. وقيل: نزلت في الأخنس بن شريق وكانت عادته الغيبة والوقيعة. وقيل: في أمية بن خلف. وقيل: في الوليد بن المغيرة واغتيابه لرسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم وغضه منه. ويجوز أن يكون السبب خاصا والوعيد عاما، ليتناول كل من باشر ذلك القبيح، وليكون جاريا مجرى التعريض بالوارد فيه، فإن ذلك أزجر له وأنكى فيه","html":"<p>Hamz means breaking, like hazm (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">هزم</bdi>); lamz (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">لمز</bdi>) means piercing. One says lamazahu (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">لمزه</bdi>) and lahazahu (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">لهزه</bdi>), “he stabbed him.” What is intended is breaking people’s reputations, diminishing them, backbiting them, and attacking them. The pattern fuʿala (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فُعَلة</bdi>) indicates that this is a habit to which the person has become addicted. Comparable forms are luʿana (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">لُعَنة</bdi>), a habitual curser, and ḍuḥaka (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ضُحَكة</bdi>), a habitual laugher. The poet says:\n“And when I am absent, you are the backbiter, the slanderer.”</p><p>The reading waylun li-l-humazati l-lumaza, “woe to the backbiter, the slanderer,” is also transmitted. Another reading has humza lumza with the mīm (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">م</bdi>) quiescent: this is the laughing-stock who produces outlandish acts and comical displays, and is laughed at and abused.</p><p>It is said to have been revealed concerning al-Akhnas ibn Sharīq, whose habit was backbiting and defamation; or concerning Umayya ibn Khalaf; or al-Walīd ibn al-Mughīra and his backbiting and disparaging God’s Messenger. The occasion may be particular while the threat is general, so as to include everyone who undertakes this ugly conduct, and to operate as an indirect allusion to the person concerning whom it was revealed. That is more restraining and more wounding to him.</p>","lemma":"","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/middle-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}},{"id":"T104.01","heading":"T104.01 — Breaking reputations and the correspondence of punishment [XVI:568–569]","text":"Meccan; nine verses. In the name of God, the All-Merciful, the Merciful.\n\nOn “hamz (همز) means breaking”: according to some, hamz (همز) is like squeezing with the hand. One says, “I squeezed the thing in my palm”; from this comes hamz (همز) in letters. Hamz of a person is backbiting him. One says hāmiz (هامز), hammāz (همّاز), and humaza of a man.\n\nOn “diminishing them,” al-Jawharī says: “Ghaḍḍa minhu yaghuḍḍu (يغضّ), with ḍamma (ضَمّة) in the imperfect, means that he lowered and diminished his standing.” Others relate to it lowering the gaze and voice, and restraining blame.\n\nOn “the pattern fuʿala (فُعَلة) indicates that this is a habit,” al-Intiṣāf says: “How fine is the correspondence of humaza and lumaza with ḥuṭama (حُطَمة)! Having marked him with this description and with a form indicating rootedness and firmness, He threatens him with this corresponding attribute, so that deed and recompense balance one another.”\n\nI say: there is another subtle correspondence. Hamz includes breaking reputations; ḥaṭm (حطم) includes breaking ribs. Nabdh, casting away, conveys contempt and belittlement, since the man imagined himself to belong among the honoured. Under His saying, ‘We seized him and his troops and cast them into the sea’ [28:40], he says: “To express contempt for them and belittle their number, He likens them to little pebbles which someone takes in his palm and throws into the sea.” Al-Wāḥidī transmits from Muqātil: “It crushes bones and consumes flesh until it assails the hearts.”","title":"Breaking reputations and the correspondence of punishment [XVI:568–569]","author":"tibi","on":["K104.02"],"anchor":"K104.02","surah":104,"ar":"مكية، تسع آيات\n\nبسم الله الرحمن الرحيم\n\nقوله: (الهُمز: الكسر)، عن بعضهم: الهمز كالعصر باليد، [يقال]: همزت الشيء في كفي، ومنه: الهمز في الحروف. وهمز الإنسان: اغتيابه، يقال: رجل هامز وهماز وهُمزة.\n\nقوله: (والغض منهم)، الجوهري: \"وغض منه يُغض بالضم، أي: وضع ونقص من قدره\". وعن غيره: منه غض الطرف والصوت: خفضهما، وغض الملامة: كفها.\n\nقوله: (وبناء فُعلة يدل على أن ذلك عادة منه)، الانتصاف: \"ما أحسن مُقابلة الهُمزة واللُّمزة بالحُطمة، لأنه لما وسمه بهذه السمة، وبما يدل على الرسوخ والتمكن، توعد فيها بهذه الصفة ليحصل التعادل بين الفعل والجزاء\".\n\nوقلت: فيه لطيفة أخرى من حيث التعادل، وهي أن الهمز فيه معنى الكسر من الأعراض، والحطم فيه معنى الكسر من الأضلاع، والنبذ فيه استحقار واستقلال، لأنه كان يزعم أنه من أهل الكرامة، قال في قوله تعالى: ﴿فَأَخَذْنَاهُ وَجُنُودَهُ فَنَبَذْنَاهُمْ فِي الْيَمِّ﴾ [القصص: 40]: \"شبههم استحقاراً لهم واستقلالاً لعددهم، بحصيات أخذهن آخذ في كفه فطرحهن في البحر\". روى الواحدي عن مقاتل: \"هي تُحطم العظام، وتأكل اللحوم حتى تهجم على القلوب\".","html":"<p>Meccan; nine verses. In the name of God, the All-Merciful, the Merciful.</p><p>On “hamz (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">همز</bdi>) means breaking”: according to some, hamz (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">همز</bdi>) is like squeezing with the hand. One says, “I squeezed the thing in my palm”; from this comes hamz (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">همز</bdi>) in letters. Hamz of a person is backbiting him. One says hāmiz (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">هامز</bdi>), hammāz (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">همّاز</bdi>), and humaza of a man.</p><p>On “diminishing them,” al-Jawharī says: “Ghaḍḍa minhu yaghuḍḍu (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">يغضّ</bdi>), with ḍamma (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ضَمّة</bdi>) in the imperfect, means that he lowered and diminished his standing.” Others relate to it lowering the gaze and voice, and restraining blame.</p><p>On “the pattern fuʿala (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فُعَلة</bdi>) indicates that this is a habit,” al-Intiṣāf says: “How fine is the correspondence of humaza and lumaza with ḥuṭama (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">حُطَمة</bdi>)! Having marked him with this description and with a form indicating rootedness and firmness, He threatens him with this corresponding attribute, so that deed and recompense balance one another.”</p><p>I say: there is another subtle correspondence. Hamz includes breaking reputations; ḥaṭm (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">حطم</bdi>) includes breaking ribs. Nabdh, casting away, conveys contempt and belittlement, since the man imagined himself to belong among the honoured. Under His saying, ‘We seized him and his troops and cast them into the sea’ [28:40], he says: “To express contempt for them and belittle their number, He likens them to little pebbles which someone takes in his palm and throws into the sea.” Al-Wāḥidī transmits from Muqātil: “It crushes bones and consumes flesh until it assails the hearts.”</p>","lemma":"الهمز: الكسر","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/middle-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}},{"id":"T104.02","heading":"T104.02 — The poetic example and strange utterances [XVI:569–570]","text":"On “And when I am absent, you are the backbiter, the slanderer”: it is said that the opening is:\n“You proffer affection for me, falsely, when you meet me.”\n\nAl-Zajjāj cites these words of Ziyād al-Aʿjam:\n“When I meet you, you bare your teeth in a smile despite your resentment;\nwhen I am absent, you are the backbiter, the slanderer.”\n\nIbn al-Sikkīt says: “Kashr means smiling. Kashara al-rajulu, iftarra, and ibtasama all mean that his teeth become visible.”\n\nOn “outlandish acts,” al-Asās says: “Among figurative uses is ‘So-and-so is devoted to awābid (أوابد) of speech,’ meaning its strange expressions, and to awābid (أوابد) of poetry, those poems whose excellence is unmatched.”","title":"The poetic example and strange utterances [XVI:569–570]","author":"tibi","on":["K104.02"],"anchor":"K104.02","surah":104,"ar":"قوله: (وإن أُغَيب فأنت الهامز اللُّمزة)، قيل: أوله:\n\nتُدلي بودي إذا لاقيتني كذباً\n\nوأنشد الزجاج لزياد الأعجم:\n\nإذا لقيتك عن سُخط تُكاشرني وإن تغيبت كنت الهامز اللُّمزه\n\nابن السِّكيت: \"الكشر: التبسم، يقال: كشر الرجل وافتر وابتسم، كل ذلك تبدو منه الأسنان\".\n\nقوله: (بالأوابد)، الأساس: \"ومن المجاز: فلان مولع بأوابد الكلام، وهي غرائبه، وبأوابد الشِّعر، وهي التي لا تُشاكل جودة\".","html":"<p>On “And when I am absent, you are the backbiter, the slanderer”: it is said that the opening is:\n“You proffer affection for me, falsely, when you meet me.”</p><p>Al-Zajjāj cites these words of Ziyād al-Aʿjam:\n“When I meet you, you bare your teeth in a smile despite your resentment;\nwhen I am absent, you are the backbiter, the slanderer.”</p><p>Ibn al-Sikkīt says: “Kashr means smiling. Kashara al-rajulu, iftarra, and ibtasama all mean that his teeth become visible.”</p><p>On “outlandish acts,” al-Asās says: “Among figurative uses is ‘So-and-so is devoted to awābid (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أوابد</bdi>) of speech,’ meaning its strange expressions, and to awābid (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أوابد</bdi>) of poetry, those poems whose excellence is unmatched.”</p>","lemma":"وإن أغيب فأنت الهامز اللمزة","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/middle-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}},{"id":"T104.03","heading":"T104.03 — General language and indirect rebuke [XVI:570–571]","text":"On “the occasion may be particular while the threat is general”: the Imām transmits from al-Farrāʾ: “An expression’s being general does not contradict a particular person’s being intended. For instance, someone says to you, ‘I have never visited you,’ and you say, ‘Everyone who has not visited me, I will not visit.’ In legal theory this is called restricting a general expression by a contextual indication of customary usage.”\n\nOn “to operate as an indirect allusion to the person concerning whom it was revealed”: if that person is al-Akhnas, Umayya, or al-Walīd, and the wording is made general as an indirect rebuke, this is more restraining and wounding. His name has not been explicitly stated so that he would put on the leopard’s hide against the person confronting him. Instead it prompts him to reflect on his own state: has he entered this general category before everyone else by backbiting the best of creation and diminishing his due? Al-Asās says: “Nakaytu fī (في) l-ʿaduwwi nikāyatan means I inflicted many wounds upon the enemy. One says, ‘So-and-so inflicts little hurt but complains at length.’”","title":"General language and indirect rebuke [XVI:570–571]","author":"tibi","on":["K104.02"],"anchor":"K104.02","surah":104,"ar":"قوله: (ويجوز أن يكون السبب خاصاً والوعيد عاماً)، روى الإمام عن الفراء أنه قال: \"كون اللفظ عاماً، لا ينافي أن يكون المراد منه شخصاً معيناً، كما أن إنساناً لو قال لك: لم أزرك أبداً، فتقول: كل من لم يزرني لا أزوره، وهو المسمى في \"أصول الفقه\" بتخصيص العام بقرينة العُرف\".\n\nقوله: (وليكون جارياً مجرى التعريض بالوارد فيه)، يعني: إذا كان الوارد منه الأخنس أو أمية أو الوليد، ويجاء باللفظ على العموم تعريضاً، كان أزجر له وأنكى فيه، إذ لم يصرح باسمه حتى يلبس لمن كافحه به جلد النمر، بل يبعثه على الفكر في أحوال نفسه، وأنه هل دخل في هذا العام أول الناس بما اغتاب به خير البرية ونقص من حقه؟ الأساس: \"نكيت في العدو نكاية: إذا أكثرت الجراح فيهم، يقال: فلان قليل النكاية طويل الشكاية\".","html":"<p>On “the occasion may be particular while the threat is general”: the Imām transmits from al-Farrāʾ: “An expression’s being general does not contradict a particular person’s being intended. For instance, someone says to you, ‘I have never visited you,’ and you say, ‘Everyone who has not visited me, I will not visit.’ In legal theory this is called restricting a general expression by a contextual indication of customary usage.”</p><p>On “to operate as an indirect allusion to the person concerning whom it was revealed”: if that person is al-Akhnas, Umayya, or al-Walīd, and the wording is made general as an indirect rebuke, this is more restraining and wounding. His name has not been explicitly stated so that he would put on the leopard’s hide against the person confronting him. Instead it prompts him to reflect on his own state: has he entered this general category before everyone else by backbiting the best of creation and diminishing his due? Al-Asās says: “Nakaytu fī (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">في</bdi>) l-ʿaduwwi nikāyatan means I inflicted many wounds upon the enemy. One says, ‘So-and-so inflicts little hurt but complains at length.’”</p>","lemma":"ويجوز أن يكون السبب خاصا والوعيد عاما","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/middle-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}}],"cairo_notes":[],"verses":[{"key":"104:1","number":1,"ar":"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَـٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ وَيْلٌ لِّكُلِّ هُمَزَةٍ لُّمَزَةٍ","en":"Woe unto every slandering traducer"}],"quran_ar":"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَـٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ وَيْلٌ لِّكُلِّ هُمَزَةٍ لُّمَزَةٍ ﴿1﴾","quran_en":"1. Woe unto every slandering traducer"},{"id":"K104.03","q":"104:2","s":104,"surah":"al-Humazah","surah_ar":"الهمزة","title":"al-Humazah · Accumulation and counting","cairo":"K IV:794–796 · T XVI:568–576","source_page_first":794,"source_page_last":796,"chunk":"passages-104","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"Accumulation and counting [IV:795]","html":"<p>“Who” is a substitute for “every,” or accusative in a construction of blame. Jammaʿa is also read with the consonant doubled, matching ʿaddadahu (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">عدّده</bdi>), “counts it over.” Another explanation takes ʿaddadahu (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">عدّده</bdi>) to mean that he makes it a reserve against the events of time.</p><p>Wa-ʿadadahu is also read: he gathers the wealth, keeps track of its amount, and counts it; or he gathers his wealth and the people who support him. This derives from saying that someone possesses ʿadad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">عَدَد</bdi>) and ʿudad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">عُدَد</bdi>) when he has a plentiful number of helpers and the resources to sustain them. It is also said that wa-ʿadadahu (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">وعَدَدَه</bdi>) means wa-ʿaddahu (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">وعدّه</bdi>), “and counted it,” with the assimilation undone, like ḍaninū (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ضَنِنُوا</bdi>) for ḍannū (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ضَنّوا</bdi>), “they were miserly.”</p>","author":"kashshaf","source_id":"K104.03"},{"type":"exposition","title":"The relative clause and the unassimilated form [XVI:571–572]","html":"<p>On “or accusative in a construction of blame”: it is said that it may be an adjective of kull, “every,” because kull is definite. Thus under “Every soul—with it a driver and a witness” [50:21], he says that “with it a driver” is circumstantial and accusative in position, referring to kull, which becomes definite through being annexed to something treated as definite.</p><p>On ḍaninū (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ضَنِنُوا</bdi>): that is, in the poet’s words:\n“Gently, reproacher! Have you learned from my character\nthat I am generous to people even when they are miserly?”</p><p>His saying “It is also said that wa-ʿadadahu (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">وعَدَدَه</bdi>) means wa-ʿaddahu (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">وعدّه</bdi>)” is coordinated with “wa-ʿadadahu (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">وعَدَدَه</bdi>): he gathers the wealth and keeps track of its amount.” On this latter analysis, it is the object of an omitted verb, as in:\n“I fed her straw and cold water.”</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T104.04"}],"types":["exposition","language","theology","reports"],"ar":"الذي بدل من كل. أو نصب على الذم. وقرئ: جمع بالتشديد، وهو مطابق لعدده. وقيل عدده جعله عدة لحوادث الدهر. وقرئ: وعدده أى جمع المال وضبط عدده وأحصاه. أو جمع ماله وقومه الذين ينصرونه، من قولك: فلان ذو عدد وعدد: إذا كان له عدد وافر من الأنصار وما يصلحهم. وقيل وعدده معناه: وعده على فك الإدغام، نحو: ضننوا\n\nقوله: (أو نصب على الذم)، قيل: يجوز أن يكون صفة لـ \"كُل\" لأنه معرفة، كما ذكر في قوله تعالى: ﴿كُلُّ نَفْسٍ مَّعَهَا سَائِقٌ وَشَهِيدٌ﴾: أن ﴿مَّعَهَا سَائِقٌ﴾ محلها النصب على الحال من ﴿كُلُّ﴾، لتعرفه بالإضافة إلى ما هو في حُكم المعرفة.\n\nقوله: (ضننوا)، أي في قول الشاعر:\n\nمهلاً أعاذل هل جربت من خلقي أني أجود لأقوام وإن ضننوا\n\nفقوله: \"وقيل: ﴿وَعَدَّدَهُ﴾، معناه: وعده\" عطف على قوله: \" ﴿وَعَدَّدَهُ﴾، أي: جمع المال وضبط عدده\" فعلى هذا: هو مفعول فعل محذوف على طريقة قوله\n\nعلفتها تبناً وماءً بارداً","layers":[{"id":"K104.03","heading":"K104.03 — Accumulation and counting [IV:795]","text":"“Who” is a substitute for “every,” or accusative in a construction of blame. Jammaʿa is also read with the consonant doubled, matching ʿaddadahu (عدّده), “counts it over.” Another explanation takes ʿaddadahu (عدّده) to mean that he makes it a reserve against the events of time.\n\nWa-ʿadadahu is also read: he gathers the wealth, keeps track of its amount, and counts it; or he gathers his wealth and the people who support him. This derives from saying that someone possesses ʿadad (عَدَد) and ʿudad (عُدَد) when he has a plentiful number of helpers and the resources to sustain them. It is also said that wa-ʿadadahu (وعَدَدَه) means wa-ʿaddahu (وعدّه), “and counted it,” with the assimilation undone, like ḍaninū (ضَنِنُوا) for ḍannū (ضَنّوا), “they were miserly.”","title":"Accumulation and counting [IV:795]","author":"kashshaf","surah":104,"ar":"الذي بدل من كل. أو نصب على الذم. وقرئ: جمع بالتشديد، وهو مطابق لعدده. وقيل عدده جعله عدة لحوادث الدهر. وقرئ: وعدده أى جمع المال وضبط عدده وأحصاه. أو جمع ماله وقومه الذين ينصرونه، من قولك: فلان ذو عدد وعدد: إذا كان له عدد وافر من الأنصار وما يصلحهم. وقيل وعدده معناه: وعده على فك الإدغام، نحو: ضننوا","html":"<p>“Who” is a substitute for “every,” or accusative in a construction of blame. Jammaʿa is also read with the consonant doubled, matching ʿaddadahu (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">عدّده</bdi>), “counts it over.” Another explanation takes ʿaddadahu (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">عدّده</bdi>) to mean that he makes it a reserve against the events of time.</p><p>Wa-ʿadadahu is also read: he gathers the wealth, keeps track of its amount, and counts it; or he gathers his wealth and the people who support him. This derives from saying that someone possesses ʿadad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">عَدَد</bdi>) and ʿudad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">عُدَد</bdi>) when he has a plentiful number of helpers and the resources to sustain them. It is also said that wa-ʿadadahu (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">وعَدَدَه</bdi>) means wa-ʿaddahu (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">وعدّه</bdi>), “and counted it,” with the assimilation undone, like ḍaninū (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ضَنِنُوا</bdi>) for ḍannū (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ضَنّوا</bdi>), “they were miserly.”</p>","lemma":"","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/middle-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}},{"id":"T104.04","heading":"T104.04 — The relative clause and the unassimilated form [XVI:571–572]","text":"On “or accusative in a construction of blame”: it is said that it may be an adjective of kull, “every,” because kull is definite. Thus under “Every soul—with it a driver and a witness” [50:21], he says that “with it a driver” is circumstantial and accusative in position, referring to kull, which becomes definite through being annexed to something treated as definite.\n\nOn ḍaninū (ضَنِنُوا): that is, in the poet’s words:\n“Gently, reproacher! Have you learned from my character\nthat I am generous to people even when they are miserly?”\n\nHis saying “It is also said that wa-ʿadadahu (وعَدَدَه) means wa-ʿaddahu (وعدّه)” is coordinated with “wa-ʿadadahu (وعَدَدَه): he gathers the wealth and keeps track of its amount.” On this latter analysis, it is the object of an omitted verb, as in:\n“I fed her straw and cold water.”","title":"The relative clause and the unassimilated form [XVI:571–572]","author":"tibi","on":["K104.03"],"anchor":"K104.03","surah":104,"ar":"قوله: (أو نصب على الذم)، قيل: يجوز أن يكون صفة لـ \"كُل\" لأنه معرفة، كما ذكر في قوله تعالى: ﴿كُلُّ نَفْسٍ مَّعَهَا سَائِقٌ وَشَهِيدٌ﴾: أن ﴿مَّعَهَا سَائِقٌ﴾ محلها النصب على الحال من ﴿كُلُّ﴾، لتعرفه بالإضافة إلى ما هو في حُكم المعرفة.\n\nقوله: (ضننوا)، أي في قول الشاعر:\n\nمهلاً أعاذل هل جربت من خلقي أني أجود لأقوام وإن ضننوا\n\nفقوله: \"وقيل: ﴿وَعَدَّدَهُ﴾، معناه: وعده\" عطف على قوله: \" ﴿وَعَدَّدَهُ﴾، أي: جمع المال وضبط عدده\" فعلى هذا: هو مفعول فعل محذوف على طريقة قوله\n\nعلفتها تبناً وماءً بارداً","html":"<p>On “or accusative in a construction of blame”: it is said that it may be an adjective of kull, “every,” because kull is definite. Thus under “Every soul—with it a driver and a witness” [50:21], he says that “with it a driver” is circumstantial and accusative in position, referring to kull, which becomes definite through being annexed to something treated as definite.</p><p>On ḍaninū (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ضَنِنُوا</bdi>): that is, in the poet’s words:\n“Gently, reproacher! Have you learned from my character\nthat I am generous to people even when they are miserly?”</p><p>His saying “It is also said that wa-ʿadadahu (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">وعَدَدَه</bdi>) means wa-ʿaddahu (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">وعدّه</bdi>)” is coordinated with “wa-ʿadadahu (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">وعَدَدَه</bdi>): he gathers the wealth and keeps track of its amount.” On this latter analysis, it is the object of an omitted verb, as in:\n“I fed her straw and cold water.”</p>","lemma":"أو نصب على الذم","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/middle-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}}],"cairo_notes":[],"verses":[{"key":"104:2","number":2,"ar":"ٱلَّذِى جَمَعَ مَالًا وَعَدَّدَهُۥ","en":"Who hath gathered wealth (of this world) and arranged it"}],"quran_ar":"ٱلَّذِى جَمَعَ مَالًا وَعَدَّدَهُۥ ﴿2﴾","quran_en":"2. Who hath gathered wealth (of this world) and arranged it"},{"id":"K104.04","q":"104:3","s":104,"surah":"al-Humazah","surah_ar":"الهمزة","title":"al-Humazah · The illusion of immortality","cairo":"K IV:794–796 · T XVI:568–576","source_page_first":794,"source_page_last":796,"chunk":"passages-104","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"The illusion of immortality [IV:795–796]","html":"<p>Akhladahu and khalladahu (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">خلّده</bdi>) have the same meaning. Wealth has lengthened his hopes and filled him with distant aspirations, until, through excessive heedlessness and long hopes, he thinks that wealth has left him immortal in this world, never to die. Or, in erecting buildings secured with stone and brick, planting trees, and cultivating the earth, he acts as someone who thinks his wealth has kept him alive.</p><p>Or it is an indirect allusion to righteous action: that is what makes its possessor abide eternally in bliss, whereas wealth has made no one abide there eternally.</p><p>It is related that al-Akhnas possessed four thousand dinars; another account says ten thousand. From al-Ḥasan: he visited a wealthy man who was ill. The man said, “What do you say of thousands which I have neither used to ransom myself from a base person nor bestowed upon an honourable one?” He asked, “But what did you keep them for?” The man said, “For the hostility of the times, the ruler’s harshness, the calamities of life, and fear of poverty.” He replied, “Then you will leave them to someone who will not praise you, and come before One who will not excuse you.”</p>","author":"kashshaf","source_id":"K104.04"},{"type":"exposition","title":"Two main readings, with three resulting explanations [XVI:572–573]","html":"<p>On “or he acts”: this is coordinated with “he thinks.” “Or it is an indirect allusion” is coordinated, in sense, with the whole explanation beginning “wealth has lengthened his hopes.” Hence the expression changes. There are thus two principal interpretations which yield three explanations.</p><p>The analysis is this: “he thinks” is circumstantial, referring to the pronoun in “amasses.” The supposition concerns either immortality in this world or everlasting bliss, as with the speaker who said, “If I am returned to my Lord, I shall certainly find a better place of return than this” [18:36], and al-ʿĀṣ ibn Wāʾil, who said, “I shall certainly be given wealth and children” [19:77]. On the first interpretation, the supposition is either literal—as intended by “he thinks that wealth has left him immortal in this world”—or figurative, as intended by “or, in erecting buildings, he acts,” just as God says, “Do you build on every elevation a monument, amusing yourselves, and take strong structures as though you would live for ever?” [26:128–129]. On the second interpretation, the verse contains an indirect allusion.</p><p>It is appropriate on the first interpretation to make “who” a substitute for “every.” The meaning is: woe to the man who amassed wealth and counted it, then entertained long hopes and fell into delusion, thinking his wealth had left him immortal in this world. On the second interpretation, it should be accusative for blame. The meaning is: woe to the impious slanderer—I mean the one whom accumulating wealth and relying on men emboldened to slander and impiety, while he also thinks his wealth will make him live eternally in bliss. “No indeed! He will surely be cast into the Crusher.” What makes its possessor abide in the lasting bliss of Paradise is righteous action, as in His saying, “The day when neither wealth nor sons avail, except one who comes to God with a sound heart” [26:88–89]. The two interpretations thus unfold what was folded together in “who is a substitute for every, or accusative in a construction of blame.” God knows best.</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T104.05"}],"types":["exposition","language","theology","reports"],"ar":"أخلده وخلده بمعنى، أى طول المال أمله، ومناه الأمانى البعيدة، حتى أصبح لفرط غفلته وطول أمله يحسب أن المال تركه خالدا في الدنيا لا يموت. أو يعمل من تشييد البنيان الموثق بالصخر والآجر وغرس الأشجار وعمارة الأرض: عمل من يظن أن ماله أبقاه حيا. أو هو تعريض بالعمل الصالح. وأنه هو الذي أخلد صاحبه في النعيم، فأما المال فما أخلد أحدا فيه. وروى أنه كان للأخنس أربعة آلاف دينار. وقيل: عشرة آلاف. وعن الحسن: أنه عاد موسرا فقال: ما تقول في ألوف لم أفتد بها من لئيم، ولا تفضلت على كريم؟ قال: ولكن لماذا؟ قال: لنبوة الزمان، وجفوة السلطان، ونوائب الدهر، ومخافة الفقر. قال: إذن تدعه لمن لا يحمدك، وترد على من لا يعذرك\n\nقوله: (أو يعمل)، عطف على قوله: \"يحسب\"، وقوله: \"أو هو تعريض\" عطف على قوله: \"أي: طول المال أمله\"، إلى آخره، من حيث المعنى. ولذلك غير العبارة؛ فهو وجهان على تقدير وجوه ثلاثة، وتقرير ذلك أن \"يحسب\" حال من الضمير في \"جمع\"، والحُسبان: إما حسبان الخلود في الدنيا، أو في النعيم أبداً، كما قال القائل: ﴿وَلَئِن رُّدِدتُّ إِلَى رَبِّي لَأَجِدَنَّ خَيْرًا مِّنْهَا مُنقَلَبًا﴾ [الكهف: 36]، وقال العاص بن وائل: ﴿وَقَالَ لَأُوتَيَنَّ مَالًا وَوَلَدًا﴾ [مريم: 77].\n\nوعلى الأول: الحُسبان إما حقيقي؛ فهو المراد من قوله: \"يحسب أن المال تركه خالداً في الدنيا\"، أو مجازي؛ فهو المعنى بقوله: \"أو يعمل من تشييد البنيان\"، كما قال تعالى: ﴿أَتَبْنُونَ بِكُلِّ رِيعٍ آيَةً تَعْبَثُونَ (وَتَتَّخِذُونَ مَصَانِعَ لَعَلَّكُمْ تَخْلُدُونَ﴾ [الشعراء: 128 - 129]. وعلى الثاني: في الآية تعريض.\n\nثم المناسب على الأول أن يجعل ﴿الَّذِي﴾ بدلاً من ﴿كُلِّ﴾، لأن المعنى: ويل للذي جمع مالاً وعدده، وطول بعد ذلك أمله ووقع في الغرور، لأنه حسب أن ماله تركه خالداً في الدنيا.\n\nوعلى الثاني أن يجعل نصباً على الذم، لأن المعنى: ويل للطاعن الفاسق، أعني: الذي جرأه على الطعن والفسق، جمع المال والاعتماد على الرجال، ومع ذلك يحسب أن ماله يُخلده في النعيم، ﴿كَلَّا لَيُنبَذَنَّ فِي الْحُطَمَةِ﴾؛ بل الذي يُخلد صاحبه في النعيم المقيم في الجنة، هو العمل الصالح، كقوله تعالى: ﴿يَوْمَ لَا يَنفَعُ مَالٌ وَلَا بَنُونَ (إِلَّا مَنْ أَتَى اللَّهَ بِقَلْبٍ سَلِيمٍ﴾ [الشعراء: 88 - 89]، فحينئذ يحصل من الوجهين نشر لما لف في قوله: \"الذي: بدل من \"كل\"، أو نصب على الذم\"، والله أعلم.","layers":[{"id":"K104.04","heading":"K104.04 — The illusion of immortality [IV:795–796]","text":"Akhladahu and khalladahu (خلّده) have the same meaning. Wealth has lengthened his hopes and filled him with distant aspirations, until, through excessive heedlessness and long hopes, he thinks that wealth has left him immortal in this world, never to die. Or, in erecting buildings secured with stone and brick, planting trees, and cultivating the earth, he acts as someone who thinks his wealth has kept him alive.\n\nOr it is an indirect allusion to righteous action: that is what makes its possessor abide eternally in bliss, whereas wealth has made no one abide there eternally.\n\nIt is related that al-Akhnas possessed four thousand dinars; another account says ten thousand. From al-Ḥasan: he visited a wealthy man who was ill. The man said, “What do you say of thousands which I have neither used to ransom myself from a base person nor bestowed upon an honourable one?” He asked, “But what did you keep them for?” The man said, “For the hostility of the times, the ruler’s harshness, the calamities of life, and fear of poverty.” He replied, “Then you will leave them to someone who will not praise you, and come before One who will not excuse you.”","title":"The illusion of immortality [IV:795–796]","author":"kashshaf","surah":104,"ar":"أخلده وخلده بمعنى، أى طول المال أمله، ومناه الأمانى البعيدة، حتى أصبح لفرط غفلته وطول أمله يحسب أن المال تركه خالدا في الدنيا لا يموت. أو يعمل من تشييد البنيان الموثق بالصخر والآجر وغرس الأشجار وعمارة الأرض: عمل من يظن أن ماله أبقاه حيا. أو هو تعريض بالعمل الصالح. وأنه هو الذي أخلد صاحبه في النعيم، فأما المال فما أخلد أحدا فيه. وروى أنه كان للأخنس أربعة آلاف دينار. وقيل: عشرة آلاف. وعن الحسن: أنه عاد موسرا فقال: ما تقول في ألوف لم أفتد بها من لئيم، ولا تفضلت على كريم؟ قال: ولكن لماذا؟ قال: لنبوة الزمان، وجفوة السلطان، ونوائب الدهر، ومخافة الفقر. قال: إذن تدعه لمن لا يحمدك، وترد على من لا يعذرك","html":"<p>Akhladahu and khalladahu (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">خلّده</bdi>) have the same meaning. Wealth has lengthened his hopes and filled him with distant aspirations, until, through excessive heedlessness and long hopes, he thinks that wealth has left him immortal in this world, never to die. Or, in erecting buildings secured with stone and brick, planting trees, and cultivating the earth, he acts as someone who thinks his wealth has kept him alive.</p><p>Or it is an indirect allusion to righteous action: that is what makes its possessor abide eternally in bliss, whereas wealth has made no one abide there eternally.</p><p>It is related that al-Akhnas possessed four thousand dinars; another account says ten thousand. From al-Ḥasan: he visited a wealthy man who was ill. The man said, “What do you say of thousands which I have neither used to ransom myself from a base person nor bestowed upon an honourable one?” He asked, “But what did you keep them for?” The man said, “For the hostility of the times, the ruler’s harshness, the calamities of life, and fear of poverty.” He replied, “Then you will leave them to someone who will not praise you, and come before One who will not excuse you.”</p>","lemma":"","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/middle-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}},{"id":"T104.05","heading":"T104.05 — Two main readings, with three resulting explanations [XVI:572–573]","text":"On “or he acts”: this is coordinated with “he thinks.” “Or it is an indirect allusion” is coordinated, in sense, with the whole explanation beginning “wealth has lengthened his hopes.” Hence the expression changes. There are thus two principal interpretations which yield three explanations.\n\nThe analysis is this: “he thinks” is circumstantial, referring to the pronoun in “amasses.” The supposition concerns either immortality in this world or everlasting bliss, as with the speaker who said, “If I am returned to my Lord, I shall certainly find a better place of return than this” [18:36], and al-ʿĀṣ ibn Wāʾil, who said, “I shall certainly be given wealth and children” [19:77]. On the first interpretation, the supposition is either literal—as intended by “he thinks that wealth has left him immortal in this world”—or figurative, as intended by “or, in erecting buildings, he acts,” just as God says, “Do you build on every elevation a monument, amusing yourselves, and take strong structures as though you would live for ever?” [26:128–129]. On the second interpretation, the verse contains an indirect allusion.\n\nIt is appropriate on the first interpretation to make “who” a substitute for “every.” The meaning is: woe to the man who amassed wealth and counted it, then entertained long hopes and fell into delusion, thinking his wealth had left him immortal in this world. On the second interpretation, it should be accusative for blame. The meaning is: woe to the impious slanderer—I mean the one whom accumulating wealth and relying on men emboldened to slander and impiety, while he also thinks his wealth will make him live eternally in bliss. “No indeed! He will surely be cast into the Crusher.” What makes its possessor abide in the lasting bliss of Paradise is righteous action, as in His saying, “The day when neither wealth nor sons avail, except one who comes to God with a sound heart” [26:88–89]. The two interpretations thus unfold what was folded together in “who is a substitute for every, or accusative in a construction of blame.” God knows best.","title":"Two main readings, with three resulting explanations [XVI:572–573]","author":"tibi","on":["K104.04"],"anchor":"K104.04","surah":104,"ar":"قوله: (أو يعمل)، عطف على قوله: \"يحسب\"، وقوله: \"أو هو تعريض\" عطف على قوله: \"أي: طول المال أمله\"، إلى آخره، من حيث المعنى. ولذلك غير العبارة؛ فهو وجهان على تقدير وجوه ثلاثة، وتقرير ذلك أن \"يحسب\" حال من الضمير في \"جمع\"، والحُسبان: إما حسبان الخلود في الدنيا، أو في النعيم أبداً، كما قال القائل: ﴿وَلَئِن رُّدِدتُّ إِلَى رَبِّي لَأَجِدَنَّ خَيْرًا مِّنْهَا مُنقَلَبًا﴾ [الكهف: 36]، وقال العاص بن وائل: ﴿وَقَالَ لَأُوتَيَنَّ مَالًا وَوَلَدًا﴾ [مريم: 77].\n\nوعلى الأول: الحُسبان إما حقيقي؛ فهو المراد من قوله: \"يحسب أن المال تركه خالداً في الدنيا\"، أو مجازي؛ فهو المعنى بقوله: \"أو يعمل من تشييد البنيان\"، كما قال تعالى: ﴿أَتَبْنُونَ بِكُلِّ رِيعٍ آيَةً تَعْبَثُونَ (وَتَتَّخِذُونَ مَصَانِعَ لَعَلَّكُمْ تَخْلُدُونَ﴾ [الشعراء: 128 - 129]. وعلى الثاني: في الآية تعريض.\n\nثم المناسب على الأول أن يجعل ﴿الَّذِي﴾ بدلاً من ﴿كُلِّ﴾، لأن المعنى: ويل للذي جمع مالاً وعدده، وطول بعد ذلك أمله ووقع في الغرور، لأنه حسب أن ماله تركه خالداً في الدنيا.\n\nوعلى الثاني أن يجعل نصباً على الذم، لأن المعنى: ويل للطاعن الفاسق، أعني: الذي جرأه على الطعن والفسق، جمع المال والاعتماد على الرجال، ومع ذلك يحسب أن ماله يُخلده في النعيم، ﴿كَلَّا لَيُنبَذَنَّ فِي الْحُطَمَةِ﴾؛ بل الذي يُخلد صاحبه في النعيم المقيم في الجنة، هو العمل الصالح، كقوله تعالى: ﴿يَوْمَ لَا يَنفَعُ مَالٌ وَلَا بَنُونَ (إِلَّا مَنْ أَتَى اللَّهَ بِقَلْبٍ سَلِيمٍ﴾ [الشعراء: 88 - 89]، فحينئذ يحصل من الوجهين نشر لما لف في قوله: \"الذي: بدل من \"كل\"، أو نصب على الذم\"، والله أعلم.","html":"<p>On “or he acts”: this is coordinated with “he thinks.” “Or it is an indirect allusion” is coordinated, in sense, with the whole explanation beginning “wealth has lengthened his hopes.” Hence the expression changes. There are thus two principal interpretations which yield three explanations.</p><p>The analysis is this: “he thinks” is circumstantial, referring to the pronoun in “amasses.” The supposition concerns either immortality in this world or everlasting bliss, as with the speaker who said, “If I am returned to my Lord, I shall certainly find a better place of return than this” [18:36], and al-ʿĀṣ ibn Wāʾil, who said, “I shall certainly be given wealth and children” [19:77]. On the first interpretation, the supposition is either literal—as intended by “he thinks that wealth has left him immortal in this world”—or figurative, as intended by “or, in erecting buildings, he acts,” just as God says, “Do you build on every elevation a monument, amusing yourselves, and take strong structures as though you would live for ever?” [26:128–129]. On the second interpretation, the verse contains an indirect allusion.</p><p>It is appropriate on the first interpretation to make “who” a substitute for “every.” The meaning is: woe to the man who amassed wealth and counted it, then entertained long hopes and fell into delusion, thinking his wealth had left him immortal in this world. On the second interpretation, it should be accusative for blame. The meaning is: woe to the impious slanderer—I mean the one whom accumulating wealth and relying on men emboldened to slander and impiety, while he also thinks his wealth will make him live eternally in bliss. “No indeed! He will surely be cast into the Crusher.” What makes its possessor abide in the lasting bliss of Paradise is righteous action, as in His saying, “The day when neither wealth nor sons avail, except one who comes to God with a sound heart” [26:88–89]. The two interpretations thus unfold what was folded together in “who is a substitute for every, or accusative in a construction of blame.” God knows best.</p>","lemma":"أو يعمل","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/middle-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}}],"cairo_notes":[],"verses":[{"key":"104:3","number":3,"ar":"يَحْسَبُ أَنَّ مَالَهُۥٓ أَخْلَدَهُۥ","en":"He thinketh that his wealth will render him immortal"}],"quran_ar":"يَحْسَبُ أَنَّ مَالَهُۥٓ أَخْلَدَهُۥ ﴿3﴾","quran_en":"3. He thinketh that his wealth will render him immortal"},{"id":"K104.05","q":"104:4–7","s":104,"surah":"al-Humazah","surah_ar":"الهمزة","title":"al-Humazah · The Crusher and the hearts","cairo":"K IV:794–796 · T XVI:568–576","source_page_first":794,"source_page_last":796,"chunk":"passages-104","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"The Crusher and the hearts [IV:796]","html":"<p>“No indeed!” restrains him from his supposition. La-yunbadhānni is also read: he and his wealth will surely be cast away. Another reading has la-yunbadhunna with ḍamma (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ضَمّة</bdi>) on the dhāl (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ذ</bdi>): he and his supporters will be cast away. La-yanbidhannahu, “he/it will surely cast him,” is also read.</p><p>“Into the Crusher”: into the fire whose nature is to crush everything thrown into it. Of a voracious man one says, “He is a crusher.” Al-ḥāṭima, “the crushing one,” is also read. The meaning is that it enters their interiors until it reaches their breasts and rises over their afʾida (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أفئدة</bdi>), the centres of their hearts. Nothing in the human body is more delicate than the fuʾād (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فؤاد</bdi>) or more acutely pained by the slightest hurt touching it. How, then, when Jahannam’s fire rises over it and takes possession of it?</p><p>The hearts may also be singled out because they are the seats of unbelief, corrupt beliefs, and evil intentions. The fire’s rising over them means that it overtops, overcomes, and encompasses them. Or, figuratively, it examines the mines from which what necessitates it is drawn.</p>","author":"kashshaf","source_id":"K104.05"},{"type":"exposition","title":"Ransoming one’s honour and the rebuke [XVI:573–574]","html":"<p>On “I have not used them to ransom myself from a base person”: I did not make my wealth a ransom for my honour, to escape his harm. He cites:\n“I protect my honour with my wealth and do not stain it;\nmay God not bless wealth once honour is lost!”</p><p>On “the hostility of the times,” al-Asās says: “Nabā ʿannī fulān means he parted from me. Between us is nabwa, estrangement. He complains of the times’ estrangement and harshness.”</p><p>On “‘No indeed!’ restrains him from his supposition,” the Imām says: “It is not as he imagined, that wealth and numbers make one immortal; rather, knowledge and righteousness do. ʿAlī, God be pleased with him, said: ‘The hoarders of wealth have died while still alive, but the learned remain as long as time remains.’ Or kallā (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">كلّا</bdi>) means ‘truly’: he will surely be cast away. The lām (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ل</bdi>) answers an oath, indicating the presence of an oath in kallā (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">كلّا</bdi>). Casting away conveys humiliation and contempt, since he imagined himself among the honoured.”</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T104.06"},{"type":"exposition","title":"The fuʾād and the fire’s discernment [XVI:574–575]","html":"<p>On “nothing in the human body is more delicate than the fuʾād (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فؤاد</bdi>),” al-Rāghib says: “Fuʾād is like qalb (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">قلب</bdi>), ‘heart,’ but it is called fuʾād (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فؤاد</bdi>) when the sense of tafaʾʾud (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">تفؤّد</bdi>), burning, is considered in it. Faʾadtu al-laḥma (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">اللحمة</bdi>) means I roasted the meat; laḥm (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">لحم</bdi>) faʾīd (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فئيد</bdi>) is roasted meat. Singling out the hearts in ‘which rises over the hearts’ calls attention to the extreme effect upon them.”</p><p>On “or, figuratively, it examines the mines from which what necessitates it is drawn”: choosing the word “mines” hints at the reverse of the Prophet’s saying, “People are mines, like mines of gold and silver.” Since these people’s hearts are the settled seats of the pollution and filth of corrupt beliefs that necessitate the Fire, and the commencement of each person’s burning is fixed according to what he deserves, it is said that the Fire figuratively examines the mines of what necessitates it.</p><p>In al-Taysīr, Abū Saʿīd says: “It knows how much punishment each of them deserves because of the unbelief and corrupt beliefs in his heart.” This comes from saying iṭṭalaʿa (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">اطّلع</bdi>) fulānun ʿalā (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">على</bdi>) amrinā: he became acquainted with our affair and knew it. God has made the fire such that it burns each person according to his deserts, neither adding nor subtracting, as though it had ascertained the amount he deserves. He says: “Since it may be described as enraged and as calling whoever turned his back and withdrew, it may also be described in this way.”</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T104.07"}],"types":["exposition","language","theology","reports"],"ar":"كلا ردع له عن حسبانه. وقرئ: لينبذان، أى: هو وماله. ولينبذن، بضم الذال، أى: هو وأنصاره. ولينبذنه في الحطمة في النار التي من شأنها أن تحطم كل ما يلقى فيها. ويقال للرجل الأكول: إنه الحطمة. وقرئ: الحاطمة، يعنى أنها تدخل في أجوافهم حتى تصل إلى صدورهم وتطلع على أفئدتهم، وهي أوساط القلوب، ولا شيء في بدن الإنسان ألطف من الفؤاد، ولا أشد تألما منه بأدنى أذى يمسه، فكيف إذا اطلعت عليه نار جهنم واستولت عليه. ويجوز أن يخص الأفئدة لأنها مواطن الكفر والعقائد الفاسدة والنيات الخبيثة. ومعنى اطلاع النار عليها: أنها تعلوها وتغلبها وتشتمل عليها. أو تطالع على سبيل المجاز معادن موجبها\n\nقوله: (لم أفتد بها من لئيم)، أي: ما جعلت مالي فداءً لعرضي منه لأسلم من أذاه، وأنشد:\n\nأصون عرضي بمالي لا أُدنسه لا بارك الله بعد العرض في المال\n\nقوله: (لنبوة الزمان)، الأساس: \"نبا عني فلان: فارقني، وبيني وبينه نبوة، وهو يشكو نبوة الزمان وجفوته\".\n\nقوله: (﴿كَلَّا﴾: ردع له عن حُسبانه)، قال الإمام: \"أي ليس كما ظن أن المال والعدد يُخلِد، بل العلم والصلاح، قال علي رضي الله عنه: \"مات خزان المال وهم أحياء والعلماء\n\nباقون ما بقي الدهر\". أو حقاً لينبذن واللام جواب القسم، فدل على حصول القسم في ﴿كَلَّا﴾، وفي النبذ الإهانة والتحقير، لأنه كان يزعم أنه من أهل الكرامة\".\n\nقوله: (ولا شيء في بدن الإنسان ألطف من الفؤاد)، الراغب: \"الفؤاد كالقلب، لكن يقال له فؤاد، إذا اعتبر فيه معنى التفؤد، أي: التوقد، يقال: فأدت اللحم: شويته، ولحم فئيد: مشوي. وتخصيص الأفئدة في قوله تعالى: ﴿تَطَّلِعُ عَلَى الْأَفْئِدَةِ﴾، تنبيه على فرط تأثير له\".\n\nقوله: (أو تُطالع على سبيل المجاز معادن مُوجبها)، وفي اختصاص لفظ \"معادن\" تلويح إلى عكس معنى قوله? : \"الناس معادن كمعادن الذهب والفضة\"، ولما كانت أفئدة هؤلاء محل مقر الرجس والخبث من العقائد الفاسدة الموجبة للنار، وأُقر بدء إحراق كل أحد على قدر استحقاقه، قيل: تطالع على المجاز معادن مُوجبها. وفي \"التيسير\": قال أبو سعيد: إنها تعلم مقدار ما يستحق كل منهم من العذاب، لما كان في قلبه من الكفر والعقائد الفاسدة، من قولك: اطلع فلان على أمرنا، أي: وقف عليه، وعَلمَه، أي: جعلها الله بحيث\n\nتحرق كل أحد على استحقاقه، لا تزيد ولا تنقص، كأنها وقفت على مبلغ استحقاقه، قال: ولما جاز وصفها بالتغيظ وبأنها تدعو من أدبر وتولى، جاز وصفها بهذا.","layers":[{"id":"K104.05","heading":"K104.05 — The Crusher and the hearts [IV:796]","text":"“No indeed!” restrains him from his supposition. La-yunbadhānni is also read: he and his wealth will surely be cast away. Another reading has la-yunbadhunna with ḍamma (ضَمّة) on the dhāl (ذ): he and his supporters will be cast away. La-yanbidhannahu, “he/it will surely cast him,” is also read.\n\n“Into the Crusher”: into the fire whose nature is to crush everything thrown into it. Of a voracious man one says, “He is a crusher.” Al-ḥāṭima, “the crushing one,” is also read. The meaning is that it enters their interiors until it reaches their breasts and rises over their afʾida (أفئدة), the centres of their hearts. Nothing in the human body is more delicate than the fuʾād (فؤاد) or more acutely pained by the slightest hurt touching it. How, then, when Jahannam’s fire rises over it and takes possession of it?\n\nThe hearts may also be singled out because they are the seats of unbelief, corrupt beliefs, and evil intentions. The fire’s rising over them means that it overtops, overcomes, and encompasses them. Or, figuratively, it examines the mines from which what necessitates it is drawn.","title":"The Crusher and the hearts [IV:796]","author":"kashshaf","surah":104,"ar":"كلا ردع له عن حسبانه. وقرئ: لينبذان، أى: هو وماله. ولينبذن، بضم الذال، أى: هو وأنصاره. ولينبذنه في الحطمة في النار التي من شأنها أن تحطم كل ما يلقى فيها. ويقال للرجل الأكول: إنه الحطمة. وقرئ: الحاطمة، يعنى أنها تدخل في أجوافهم حتى تصل إلى صدورهم وتطلع على أفئدتهم، وهي أوساط القلوب، ولا شيء في بدن الإنسان ألطف من الفؤاد، ولا أشد تألما منه بأدنى أذى يمسه، فكيف إذا اطلعت عليه نار جهنم واستولت عليه. ويجوز أن يخص الأفئدة لأنها مواطن الكفر والعقائد الفاسدة والنيات الخبيثة. ومعنى اطلاع النار عليها: أنها تعلوها وتغلبها وتشتمل عليها. أو تطالع على سبيل المجاز معادن موجبها","html":"<p>“No indeed!” restrains him from his supposition. La-yunbadhānni is also read: he and his wealth will surely be cast away. Another reading has la-yunbadhunna with ḍamma (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ضَمّة</bdi>) on the dhāl (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ذ</bdi>): he and his supporters will be cast away. La-yanbidhannahu, “he/it will surely cast him,” is also read.</p><p>“Into the Crusher”: into the fire whose nature is to crush everything thrown into it. Of a voracious man one says, “He is a crusher.” Al-ḥāṭima, “the crushing one,” is also read. The meaning is that it enters their interiors until it reaches their breasts and rises over their afʾida (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أفئدة</bdi>), the centres of their hearts. Nothing in the human body is more delicate than the fuʾād (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فؤاد</bdi>) or more acutely pained by the slightest hurt touching it. How, then, when Jahannam’s fire rises over it and takes possession of it?</p><p>The hearts may also be singled out because they are the seats of unbelief, corrupt beliefs, and evil intentions. The fire’s rising over them means that it overtops, overcomes, and encompasses them. Or, figuratively, it examines the mines from which what necessitates it is drawn.</p>","lemma":"","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/middle-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}},{"id":"T104.06","heading":"T104.06 — Ransoming one’s honour and the rebuke [XVI:573–574]","text":"On “I have not used them to ransom myself from a base person”: I did not make my wealth a ransom for my honour, to escape his harm. He cites:\n“I protect my honour with my wealth and do not stain it;\nmay God not bless wealth once honour is lost!”\n\nOn “the hostility of the times,” al-Asās says: “Nabā ʿannī fulān means he parted from me. Between us is nabwa, estrangement. He complains of the times’ estrangement and harshness.”\n\nOn “‘No indeed!’ restrains him from his supposition,” the Imām says: “It is not as he imagined, that wealth and numbers make one immortal; rather, knowledge and righteousness do. ʿAlī, God be pleased with him, said: ‘The hoarders of wealth have died while still alive, but the learned remain as long as time remains.’ Or kallā (كلّا) means ‘truly’: he will surely be cast away. The lām (ل) answers an oath, indicating the presence of an oath in kallā (كلّا). Casting away conveys humiliation and contempt, since he imagined himself among the honoured.”","title":"Ransoming one’s honour and the rebuke [XVI:573–574]","author":"tibi","on":["K104.04","K104.05"],"anchor":"K104.05","surah":104,"ar":"قوله: (لم أفتد بها من لئيم)، أي: ما جعلت مالي فداءً لعرضي منه لأسلم من أذاه، وأنشد:\n\nأصون عرضي بمالي لا أُدنسه لا بارك الله بعد العرض في المال\n\nقوله: (لنبوة الزمان)، الأساس: \"نبا عني فلان: فارقني، وبيني وبينه نبوة، وهو يشكو نبوة الزمان وجفوته\".\n\nقوله: (﴿كَلَّا﴾: ردع له عن حُسبانه)، قال الإمام: \"أي ليس كما ظن أن المال والعدد يُخلِد، بل العلم والصلاح، قال علي رضي الله عنه: \"مات خزان المال وهم أحياء والعلماء\n\nباقون ما بقي الدهر\". أو حقاً لينبذن واللام جواب القسم، فدل على حصول القسم في ﴿كَلَّا﴾، وفي النبذ الإهانة والتحقير، لأنه كان يزعم أنه من أهل الكرامة\".","html":"<p>On “I have not used them to ransom myself from a base person”: I did not make my wealth a ransom for my honour, to escape his harm. He cites:\n“I protect my honour with my wealth and do not stain it;\nmay God not bless wealth once honour is lost!”</p><p>On “the hostility of the times,” al-Asās says: “Nabā ʿannī fulān means he parted from me. Between us is nabwa, estrangement. He complains of the times’ estrangement and harshness.”</p><p>On “‘No indeed!’ restrains him from his supposition,” the Imām says: “It is not as he imagined, that wealth and numbers make one immortal; rather, knowledge and righteousness do. ʿAlī, God be pleased with him, said: ‘The hoarders of wealth have died while still alive, but the learned remain as long as time remains.’ Or kallā (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">كلّا</bdi>) means ‘truly’: he will surely be cast away. The lām (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ل</bdi>) answers an oath, indicating the presence of an oath in kallā (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">كلّا</bdi>). Casting away conveys humiliation and contempt, since he imagined himself among the honoured.”</p>","lemma":"لم أفتد بها من لئيم","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/middle-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}},{"id":"T104.07","heading":"T104.07 — The fuʾād and the fire’s discernment [XVI:574–575]","text":"On “nothing in the human body is more delicate than the fuʾād (فؤاد),” al-Rāghib says: “Fuʾād is like qalb (قلب), ‘heart,’ but it is called fuʾād (فؤاد) when the sense of tafaʾʾud (تفؤّد), burning, is considered in it. Faʾadtu al-laḥma (اللحمة) means I roasted the meat; laḥm (لحم) faʾīd (فئيد) is roasted meat. Singling out the hearts in ‘which rises over the hearts’ calls attention to the extreme effect upon them.”\n\nOn “or, figuratively, it examines the mines from which what necessitates it is drawn”: choosing the word “mines” hints at the reverse of the Prophet’s saying, “People are mines, like mines of gold and silver.” Since these people’s hearts are the settled seats of the pollution and filth of corrupt beliefs that necessitate the Fire, and the commencement of each person’s burning is fixed according to what he deserves, it is said that the Fire figuratively examines the mines of what necessitates it.\n\nIn al-Taysīr, Abū Saʿīd says: “It knows how much punishment each of them deserves because of the unbelief and corrupt beliefs in his heart.” This comes from saying iṭṭalaʿa (اطّلع) fulānun ʿalā (على) amrinā: he became acquainted with our affair and knew it. God has made the fire such that it burns each person according to his deserts, neither adding nor subtracting, as though it had ascertained the amount he deserves. He says: “Since it may be described as enraged and as calling whoever turned his back and withdrew, it may also be described in this way.”","title":"The fuʾād and the fire’s discernment [XVI:574–575]","author":"tibi","on":["K104.05"],"anchor":"K104.05","surah":104,"ar":"قوله: (ولا شيء في بدن الإنسان ألطف من الفؤاد)، الراغب: \"الفؤاد كالقلب، لكن يقال له فؤاد، إذا اعتبر فيه معنى التفؤد، أي: التوقد، يقال: فأدت اللحم: شويته، ولحم فئيد: مشوي. وتخصيص الأفئدة في قوله تعالى: ﴿تَطَّلِعُ عَلَى الْأَفْئِدَةِ﴾، تنبيه على فرط تأثير له\".\n\nقوله: (أو تُطالع على سبيل المجاز معادن مُوجبها)، وفي اختصاص لفظ \"معادن\" تلويح إلى عكس معنى قوله? : \"الناس معادن كمعادن الذهب والفضة\"، ولما كانت أفئدة هؤلاء محل مقر الرجس والخبث من العقائد الفاسدة الموجبة للنار، وأُقر بدء إحراق كل أحد على قدر استحقاقه، قيل: تطالع على المجاز معادن مُوجبها. وفي \"التيسير\": قال أبو سعيد: إنها تعلم مقدار ما يستحق كل منهم من العذاب، لما كان في قلبه من الكفر والعقائد الفاسدة، من قولك: اطلع فلان على أمرنا، أي: وقف عليه، وعَلمَه، أي: جعلها الله بحيث\n\nتحرق كل أحد على استحقاقه، لا تزيد ولا تنقص، كأنها وقفت على مبلغ استحقاقه، قال: ولما جاز وصفها بالتغيظ وبأنها تدعو من أدبر وتولى، جاز وصفها بهذا.","html":"<p>On “nothing in the human body is more delicate than the fuʾād (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فؤاد</bdi>),” al-Rāghib says: “Fuʾād is like qalb (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">قلب</bdi>), ‘heart,’ but it is called fuʾād (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فؤاد</bdi>) when the sense of tafaʾʾud (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">تفؤّد</bdi>), burning, is considered in it. Faʾadtu al-laḥma (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">اللحمة</bdi>) means I roasted the meat; laḥm (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">لحم</bdi>) faʾīd (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فئيد</bdi>) is roasted meat. Singling out the hearts in ‘which rises over the hearts’ calls attention to the extreme effect upon them.”</p><p>On “or, figuratively, it examines the mines from which what necessitates it is drawn”: choosing the word “mines” hints at the reverse of the Prophet’s saying, “People are mines, like mines of gold and silver.” Since these people’s hearts are the settled seats of the pollution and filth of corrupt beliefs that necessitate the Fire, and the commencement of each person’s burning is fixed according to what he deserves, it is said that the Fire figuratively examines the mines of what necessitates it.</p><p>In al-Taysīr, Abū Saʿīd says: “It knows how much punishment each of them deserves because of the unbelief and corrupt beliefs in his heart.” This comes from saying iṭṭalaʿa (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">اطّلع</bdi>) fulānun ʿalā (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">على</bdi>) amrinā: he became acquainted with our affair and knew it. God has made the fire such that it burns each person according to his deserts, neither adding nor subtracting, as though it had ascertained the amount he deserves. He says: “Since it may be described as enraged and as calling whoever turned his back and withdrew, it may also be described in this way.”</p>","lemma":"ولا شيء في بدن الإنسان ألطف من الفؤاد","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/middle-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}}],"cairo_notes":[],"verses":[{"key":"104:4","number":4,"ar":"كَلَّا ۖ لَيُنۢبَذَنَّ فِى ٱلْحُطَمَةِ","en":"Nay, but verily he will be flung to the Consuming One"},{"key":"104:5","number":5,"ar":"وَمَآ أَدْرَىٰكَ مَا ٱلْحُطَمَةُ","en":"Ah, what will convey unto thee what the Consuming One is"},{"key":"104:6","number":6,"ar":"نَارُ ٱللَّهِ ٱلْمُوقَدَةُ","en":"(It is) the fire of Allah, kindled"},{"key":"104:7","number":7,"ar":"ٱلَّتِى تَطَّلِعُ عَلَى ٱلْأَفْـِٔدَةِ","en":"Which leapeth up over the hearts (of men)"}],"quran_ar":"كَلَّا ۖ لَيُنۢبَذَنَّ فِى ٱلْحُطَمَةِ ﴿4﴾ وَمَآ أَدْرَىٰكَ مَا ٱلْحُطَمَةُ ﴿5﴾ نَارُ ٱللَّهِ ٱلْمُوقَدَةُ ﴿6﴾ ٱلَّتِى تَطَّلِعُ عَلَى ٱلْأَفْـِٔدَةِ ﴿7﴾","quran_en":"4. Nay, but verily he will be flung to the Consuming One 5. Ah, what will convey unto thee what the Consuming One is 6. (It is) the fire of Allah, kindled 7. Which leapeth up over the hearts (of men)"},{"id":"K104.06","q":"104:8–9","s":104,"surah":"al-Humazah","surah_ar":"الهمزة","title":"al-Humazah · Closed doors and extended pillars","cairo":"K IV:794–796 · T XVI:568–576","source_page_first":794,"source_page_last":796,"chunk":"passages-104","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"Closed doors and extended pillars [IV:796]","html":"<p>“Closed”: shut over them. The poet says:\n“My she-camel longs for the mountains of Mecca,\nwhile before her the gates of Ṣanʿāʾ are closed.”</p><p>Fī ʿumud (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">عُمُد</bdi>) is read with two ḍammas (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ضَمَّات</bdi> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ـُ</bdi>)); ʿumd (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">عُمْد</bdi>) with the mīm (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">م</bdi>) quiescent; and ʿamad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">عَمَد</bdi>) with two fatḥas (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فَتَحَات</bdi> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ـَ</bdi>)). The meaning is that their despair of escape and certainty of everlasting imprisonment are reinforced: the doors are closed upon them and pillars stretched across the doors, fastening upon fastening.</p><p>Or the meaning is that it is closed over them while they are secured in extended pillars, like the stocks in which thieves are confined. God, protect us from the Fire, O best of those in whom refuge is sought!</p>","author":"kashshaf","source_id":"K104.06"},{"type":"exposition","title":"Closure and the readings of “pillars” [XVI:575]","html":"<p>On “muʾṣada (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">مؤصدة</bdi>): closed,” al-Rāghib says: “A waṣīda (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">وصيدة</bdi>) is a chamber made in a mountain for storing property. One says awṣadtu al-bāba (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أوصدت الباب</bdi>) and āṣadtuhu (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">آصدته</bdi>): I closed the door and secured it. God Most High says, ‘Over them is a closed fire’ [90:20]; it is also read with hamza (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">همزة</bdi>).”</p><p>On “fī (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">في</bdi>) ʿumud (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">عُمُد</bdi>) is read”: Abū Bakr, Ḥamza, and al-Kisāʾī read it with two ḍammas (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ضَمَّات</bdi> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ـُ</bdi>)); the others with two fatḥas (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فَتَحَات</bdi> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ـَ</bdi>)).</p><p>On “pillars are stretched across the doors”: it is said that, on this interpretation, fī (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">في</bdi>) ʿumud (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">عُمُد</bdi>) is circumstantial, referring to the pronoun in muʾṣada (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">مؤصدة</bdi>)—namely the one referring to the doors. On the interpretation “secured in pillars,” it is circumstantial, referring to the pronoun in ʿalayhim (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">عليهم</bdi>), “over them.”</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T104.08"},{"type":"exposition","title":"Stocks, the particular occasion, and the general scope [XVI:576]","html":"<p>On “like the stocks,” al-Jawharī says: “The miqṭara (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">مقطرة</bdi>) is the falaq (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فلق</bdi>): a piece of wood with holes into which prisoners’ legs are inserted.”</p><p>I say: the first interpretation suits the report that the verse was revealed concerning al-Akhnas ibn Sharīq, Umayya ibn Khalaf, or al-Walīd ibn al-Mughīra and his backbiting God’s Messenger. Having explained that the Crusher is the fire which examines the mines of what necessitates it, God follows this with “It is closed over them.” That is, the fire has ascertained these people’s deserts for backbiting the best of humankind, and is therefore closed and shut over them. Their despair of escape and certainty of everlasting imprisonment are thus reinforced.</p><p>The second interpretation suits taking “every backbiter and slanderer” generally. This is indicated by his saying, “the laughing-stock who produces outlandish acts and comical displays,” because he attacks people’s reputations like a thief stealing their property. On this interpretation, their remaining for ever in the Fire would follow.</p><p>End of the sūrah.</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T104.09"}],"types":["exposition","language","theology","reports"],"ar":"مؤصدة مطبقا. قال: تحن إلى أجبال مكة ناقتي ... ومن دونها أبواب صنعاء مؤصدة وقرئ: في عمد، بضمتين. وعمد، بسكون الميم. وعمد. بفتحتين. والمعنى: أنه يؤكد يأسهم من الخروج وتيقنهم بحبس الأبد، فتؤصد عليهم الأبواب وتمدد على الأبواب العمد، استيثاقا في استيثاق. ويجوز أن يكون المعنى: أنها عليهم مؤصدة، موثقين في عمد ممددة مثل المقاطر التي تقطر فيها اللصوص. اللهم أجرنا من النار يا خير مستجار.\n\nقوله: (﴿مُّؤْصَدَةٌ﴾: مطبقة)، الراغب: \"الوصيدة: حجرة تجعل للمال في الجبل، يقال: أوصدت الباب وآصدته: أطبقته وأحكمته، قال تعالى: (﴿عَلَيْهِمْ نَارٌ مُّؤْصَدَةٌ﴾، وقرئ بالهمز\".\n\nقوله: (وقرئ: \"في عُمُد\")، أبو بكر وحمزة والكسائي: بضمتين، والباقون: بفتحتين.\n\nقوله: (وتُمدد على الأبوا العُمُد)، قيل: على هذا: ﴿فِي عَمَدٍ﴾ حال من الضمير في ﴿مُّؤْصَدَةٌ﴾، أعني العائد إلى الأبواب، وعلى قوله: \"موثقين في عمد\": حال من الضمير في: ﴿عَلَيْهِمْ﴾.\n\nقوله: (مثل المقاطر)، الجوهري: \"المقطرة وهي الفلق، وهي خشبة فيها خروق تدخل فيها أرجل المحبوسين\". وقلت: الوجه الأول مناسب لما روي أن الآية نزلت في أخنس بن شريق، أو أمية بن خلف، أو الوليد بن المغيرة واغتيابه لرسول الله? ؛ فإنه تعالى لما بين أن ﴿الْحُطَمَةِ﴾، هي النار التي تطالع معادن موجبها، أتبعه قوله: ﴿إِنَّهَا عَلَيْهِم مُّؤْصَدَةٌ﴾، أي: النار طالعت على استحقاق هؤلاء بسبب اغتيابهم خير البشر، فكانت عليهم مؤصدة مطبقة، فأكد يأسهم من الخروج، وتيقنهم بحبس الأبد.\n\nوالثاني موافق لأن يراد بقوله: ﴿لِّكُلِّ هُمَزَةٍ لُّمَزَةٍ﴾ العموم، وهو المشار إليه بقوله: \"وهو المسخرة الذي يأتي بالأوابد والأضاحيك\"، لأنه يطعن في أعراض الناس، كاللص الذي يسرق أموالهم؛ فعلى هذا، يلزم خلودهم في النار.\n\nتمت السورة","layers":[{"id":"K104.06","heading":"K104.06 — Closed doors and extended pillars [IV:796]","text":"“Closed”: shut over them. The poet says:\n“My she-camel longs for the mountains of Mecca,\nwhile before her the gates of Ṣanʿāʾ are closed.”\n\nFī ʿumud (عُمُد) is read with two ḍammas (ضَمَّات (ـُ)); ʿumd (عُمْد) with the mīm (م) quiescent; and ʿamad (عَمَد) with two fatḥas (فَتَحَات (ـَ)). The meaning is that their despair of escape and certainty of everlasting imprisonment are reinforced: the doors are closed upon them and pillars stretched across the doors, fastening upon fastening.\n\nOr the meaning is that it is closed over them while they are secured in extended pillars, like the stocks in which thieves are confined. God, protect us from the Fire, O best of those in whom refuge is sought!","title":"Closed doors and extended pillars [IV:796]","author":"kashshaf","surah":104,"ar":"مؤصدة مطبقا. قال: تحن إلى أجبال مكة ناقتي ... ومن دونها أبواب صنعاء مؤصدة وقرئ: في عمد، بضمتين. وعمد، بسكون الميم. وعمد. بفتحتين. والمعنى: أنه يؤكد يأسهم من الخروج وتيقنهم بحبس الأبد، فتؤصد عليهم الأبواب وتمدد على الأبواب العمد، استيثاقا في استيثاق. ويجوز أن يكون المعنى: أنها عليهم مؤصدة، موثقين في عمد ممددة مثل المقاطر التي تقطر فيها اللصوص. اللهم أجرنا من النار يا خير مستجار.","html":"<p>“Closed”: shut over them. The poet says:\n“My she-camel longs for the mountains of Mecca,\nwhile before her the gates of Ṣanʿāʾ are closed.”</p><p>Fī ʿumud (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">عُمُد</bdi>) is read with two ḍammas (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ضَمَّات</bdi> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ـُ</bdi>)); ʿumd (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">عُمْد</bdi>) with the mīm (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">م</bdi>) quiescent; and ʿamad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">عَمَد</bdi>) with two fatḥas (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فَتَحَات</bdi> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ـَ</bdi>)). The meaning is that their despair of escape and certainty of everlasting imprisonment are reinforced: the doors are closed upon them and pillars stretched across the doors, fastening upon fastening.</p><p>Or the meaning is that it is closed over them while they are secured in extended pillars, like the stocks in which thieves are confined. God, protect us from the Fire, O best of those in whom refuge is sought!</p>","lemma":"","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/middle-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}},{"id":"T104.08","heading":"T104.08 — Closure and the readings of “pillars” [XVI:575]","text":"On “muʾṣada (مؤصدة): closed,” al-Rāghib says: “A waṣīda (وصيدة) is a chamber made in a mountain for storing property. One says awṣadtu al-bāba (أوصدت الباب) and āṣadtuhu (آصدته): I closed the door and secured it. God Most High says, ‘Over them is a closed fire’ [90:20]; it is also read with hamza (همزة).”\n\nOn “fī (في) ʿumud (عُمُد) is read”: Abū Bakr, Ḥamza, and al-Kisāʾī read it with two ḍammas (ضَمَّات (ـُ)); the others with two fatḥas (فَتَحَات (ـَ)).\n\nOn “pillars are stretched across the doors”: it is said that, on this interpretation, fī (في) ʿumud (عُمُد) is circumstantial, referring to the pronoun in muʾṣada (مؤصدة)—namely the one referring to the doors. On the interpretation “secured in pillars,” it is circumstantial, referring to the pronoun in ʿalayhim (عليهم), “over them.”","title":"Closure and the readings of “pillars” [XVI:575]","author":"tibi","on":["K104.06"],"anchor":"K104.06","surah":104,"ar":"قوله: (﴿مُّؤْصَدَةٌ﴾: مطبقة)، الراغب: \"الوصيدة: حجرة تجعل للمال في الجبل، يقال: أوصدت الباب وآصدته: أطبقته وأحكمته، قال تعالى: (﴿عَلَيْهِمْ نَارٌ مُّؤْصَدَةٌ﴾، وقرئ بالهمز\".\n\nقوله: (وقرئ: \"في عُمُد\")، أبو بكر وحمزة والكسائي: بضمتين، والباقون: بفتحتين.\n\nقوله: (وتُمدد على الأبوا العُمُد)، قيل: على هذا: ﴿فِي عَمَدٍ﴾ حال من الضمير في ﴿مُّؤْصَدَةٌ﴾، أعني العائد إلى الأبواب، وعلى قوله: \"موثقين في عمد\": حال من الضمير في: ﴿عَلَيْهِمْ﴾.","html":"<p>On “muʾṣada (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">مؤصدة</bdi>): closed,” al-Rāghib says: “A waṣīda (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">وصيدة</bdi>) is a chamber made in a mountain for storing property. One says awṣadtu al-bāba (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أوصدت الباب</bdi>) and āṣadtuhu (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">آصدته</bdi>): I closed the door and secured it. God Most High says, ‘Over them is a closed fire’ [90:20]; it is also read with hamza (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">همزة</bdi>).”</p><p>On “fī (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">في</bdi>) ʿumud (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">عُمُد</bdi>) is read”: Abū Bakr, Ḥamza, and al-Kisāʾī read it with two ḍammas (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ضَمَّات</bdi> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ـُ</bdi>)); the others with two fatḥas (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فَتَحَات</bdi> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ـَ</bdi>)).</p><p>On “pillars are stretched across the doors”: it is said that, on this interpretation, fī (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">في</bdi>) ʿumud (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">عُمُد</bdi>) is circumstantial, referring to the pronoun in muʾṣada (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">مؤصدة</bdi>)—namely the one referring to the doors. On the interpretation “secured in pillars,” it is circumstantial, referring to the pronoun in ʿalayhim (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">عليهم</bdi>), “over them.”</p>","lemma":"","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/middle-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}},{"id":"T104.09","heading":"T104.09 — Stocks, the particular occasion, and the general scope [XVI:576]","text":"On “like the stocks,” al-Jawharī says: “The miqṭara (مقطرة) is the falaq (فلق): a piece of wood with holes into which prisoners’ legs are inserted.”\n\nI say: the first interpretation suits the report that the verse was revealed concerning al-Akhnas ibn Sharīq, Umayya ibn Khalaf, or al-Walīd ibn al-Mughīra and his backbiting God’s Messenger. Having explained that the Crusher is the fire which examines the mines of what necessitates it, God follows this with “It is closed over them.” That is, the fire has ascertained these people’s deserts for backbiting the best of humankind, and is therefore closed and shut over them. Their despair of escape and certainty of everlasting imprisonment are thus reinforced.\n\nThe second interpretation suits taking “every backbiter and slanderer” generally. This is indicated by his saying, “the laughing-stock who produces outlandish acts and comical displays,” because he attacks people’s reputations like a thief stealing their property. On this interpretation, their remaining for ever in the Fire would follow.\n\nEnd of the sūrah.","title":"Stocks, the particular occasion, and the general scope [XVI:576]","author":"tibi","on":["K104.06"],"anchor":"K104.06","surah":104,"ar":"قوله: (مثل المقاطر)، الجوهري: \"المقطرة وهي الفلق، وهي خشبة فيها خروق تدخل فيها أرجل المحبوسين\". وقلت: الوجه الأول مناسب لما روي أن الآية نزلت في أخنس بن شريق، أو أمية بن خلف، أو الوليد بن المغيرة واغتيابه لرسول الله? ؛ فإنه تعالى لما بين أن ﴿الْحُطَمَةِ﴾، هي النار التي تطالع معادن موجبها، أتبعه قوله: ﴿إِنَّهَا عَلَيْهِم مُّؤْصَدَةٌ﴾، أي: النار طالعت على استحقاق هؤلاء بسبب اغتيابهم خير البشر، فكانت عليهم مؤصدة مطبقة، فأكد يأسهم من الخروج، وتيقنهم بحبس الأبد.\n\nوالثاني موافق لأن يراد بقوله: ﴿لِّكُلِّ هُمَزَةٍ لُّمَزَةٍ﴾ العموم، وهو المشار إليه بقوله: \"وهو المسخرة الذي يأتي بالأوابد والأضاحيك\"، لأنه يطعن في أعراض الناس، كاللص الذي يسرق أموالهم؛ فعلى هذا، يلزم خلودهم في النار.\n\nتمت السورة","html":"<p>On “like the stocks,” al-Jawharī says: “The miqṭara (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">مقطرة</bdi>) is the falaq (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فلق</bdi>): a piece of wood with holes into which prisoners’ legs are inserted.”</p><p>I say: the first interpretation suits the report that the verse was revealed concerning al-Akhnas ibn Sharīq, Umayya ibn Khalaf, or al-Walīd ibn al-Mughīra and his backbiting God’s Messenger. Having explained that the Crusher is the fire which examines the mines of what necessitates it, God follows this with “It is closed over them.” That is, the fire has ascertained these people’s deserts for backbiting the best of humankind, and is therefore closed and shut over them. Their despair of escape and certainty of everlasting imprisonment are thus reinforced.</p><p>The second interpretation suits taking “every backbiter and slanderer” generally. This is indicated by his saying, “the laughing-stock who produces outlandish acts and comical displays,” because he attacks people’s reputations like a thief stealing their property. On this interpretation, their remaining for ever in the Fire would follow.</p><p>End of the sūrah.</p>","lemma":"مثل المقاطر","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/middle-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}}],"cairo_notes":[],"verses":[{"key":"104:8","number":8,"ar":"إِنَّهَا عَلَيْهِم مُّؤْصَدَةٌ","en":"Lo! it is closed in on them"},{"key":"104:9","number":9,"ar":"فِى عَمَدٍ مُّمَدَّدَةٍۭ","en":"In outstretched columns"}],"quran_ar":"إِنَّهَا عَلَيْهِم مُّؤْصَدَةٌ ﴿8﴾ فِى عَمَدٍ مُّمَدَّدَةٍۭ ﴿9﴾","quran_en":"8. Lo! it is closed in on them 9. In outstretched columns"},{"id":"K104.07","q":"104:1–9","s":104,"surah":"al-Humazah","surah_ar":"الهمزة","title":"al-Humazah · Closing report","cairo":"K IV:794–796 · T XVI:568–576","source_page_first":794,"source_page_last":796,"chunk":"passages-104","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"Closing report [IV:796]","html":"<p>From God’s Messenger: “Whoever recites Sūrat al-Humazah, God gives him ten good deeds for every person who mocked Muḥammad and his companions.”</p>","author":"kashshaf","source_id":"K104.07"}],"types":["exposition","language","theology","reports"],"ar":"عن رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم: «من قرأ سورة الهمزة أعطاه الله عشر حسنات بعدد من استهزا بمحمد وأصحابه» .","layers":[{"id":"K104.07","heading":"K104.07 — Closing report [IV:796]","text":"From God’s Messenger: “Whoever recites Sūrat al-Humazah, God gives him ten good deeds for every person who mocked Muḥammad and his companions.”","title":"Closing report [IV:796]","author":"kashshaf","surah":104,"ar":"عن رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم: «من قرأ سورة الهمزة أعطاه الله عشر حسنات بعدد من استهزا بمحمد وأصحابه» .","html":"<p>From God’s Messenger: “Whoever recites Sūrat al-Humazah, God gives him ten good deeds for every person who mocked Muḥammad and his companions.”</p>","lemma":"","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/middle-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}}],"cairo_notes":[],"verses":[{"key":"104:1","number":1,"ar":"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَـٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ وَيْلٌ لِّكُلِّ هُمَزَةٍ لُّمَزَةٍ","en":"Woe unto every slandering traducer"},{"key":"104:2","number":2,"ar":"ٱلَّذِى جَمَعَ مَالًا وَعَدَّدَهُۥ","en":"Who hath gathered wealth (of this world) and arranged it"},{"key":"104:3","number":3,"ar":"يَحْسَبُ أَنَّ مَالَهُۥٓ أَخْلَدَهُۥ","en":"He thinketh that his wealth will render him immortal"},{"key":"104:4","number":4,"ar":"كَلَّا ۖ لَيُنۢبَذَنَّ فِى ٱلْحُطَمَةِ","en":"Nay, but verily he will be flung to the Consuming One"},{"key":"104:5","number":5,"ar":"وَمَآ أَدْرَىٰكَ مَا ٱلْحُطَمَةُ","en":"Ah, what will convey unto thee what the Consuming One is"},{"key":"104:6","number":6,"ar":"نَارُ ٱللَّهِ ٱلْمُوقَدَةُ","en":"(It is) the fire of Allah, kindled"},{"key":"104:7","number":7,"ar":"ٱلَّتِى تَطَّلِعُ عَلَى ٱلْأَفْـِٔدَةِ","en":"Which leapeth up over the hearts (of men)"},{"key":"104:8","number":8,"ar":"إِنَّهَا عَلَيْهِم مُّؤْصَدَةٌ","en":"Lo! it is closed in on them"},{"key":"104:9","number":9,"ar":"فِى عَمَدٍ مُّمَدَّدَةٍۭ","en":"In outstretched columns"}],"quran_ar":"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَـٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ وَيْلٌ لِّكُلِّ هُمَزَةٍ لُّمَزَةٍ ﴿1﴾ ٱلَّذِى جَمَعَ مَالًا وَعَدَّدَهُۥ ﴿2﴾ يَحْسَبُ أَنَّ مَالَهُۥٓ أَخْلَدَهُۥ ﴿3﴾ كَلَّا ۖ لَيُنۢبَذَنَّ فِى ٱلْحُطَمَةِ ﴿4﴾ وَمَآ أَدْرَىٰكَ مَا ٱلْحُطَمَةُ ﴿5﴾ نَارُ ٱللَّهِ ٱلْمُوقَدَةُ ﴿6﴾ ٱلَّتِى تَطَّلِعُ عَلَى ٱلْأَفْـِٔدَةِ ﴿7﴾ إِنَّهَا عَلَيْهِم مُّؤْصَدَةٌ ﴿8﴾ فِى عَمَدٍ مُّمَدَّدَةٍۭ ﴿9﴾","quran_en":"1. Woe unto every slandering traducer 2. Who hath gathered wealth (of this world) and arranged it 3. He thinketh that his wealth will render him immortal 4. Nay, but verily he will be flung to the Consuming One 5. Ah, what will convey unto thee what the Consuming One is 6. (It is) the fire of Allah, kindled 7. Which leapeth up over the hearts (of men) 8. Lo! it is closed in on them 9. In outstretched columns"}];
